        {
            "number": 5,
            "revision": "f32168711aade98e97e25215a3d11d9d2ce292b7",
            "parents": [
                "688a51364c3d6dd3db13ab6081c836dc14d32f73"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/67/82867/5",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Patrick Williams",
                "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                "username": "williamspatrick"
            },
            "createdOn": 1756399463,
            "author": {
                "name": "Robert Ercean Dima",
                "email": "robert3@meta.com",
                "username": "robert-ercean"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This is going to cause any application that expects to write data on shutdown to crash/fail to write.  What is the advantage to this instead of simply shutting down the services and unmounting the persistent dir?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't know a great way to hook this into systemd shutdown.  It isn't doing BMC power offs, but is sending a magic command to the hotswap controller to reset it.  We only want to do this when we explicitly are doing a full-chassis-cycle and not any BMC reboot.\n\nWe're only still going to be up momentarily anyhow, but it seems like the overlay itself stays read-write.  This is better than the alternative we were seeing which is that we were ending up with a decent amount of ubifs corruption (which is a kernel bug to begin with...)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Script itself is fine.  I suspect this is just a matter of needing slde_cycle to be hooked into systemd shutdown routines a little?  But overall, this is only functionally a change to Yosemite, so it's fine."
                }
            ],
            "files": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "type": "ADDED",
                    "insertions": 28,
                    "deletions": 0
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-yosemite4/recipes-phosphor/state/phosphor-state-manager/chassis-powercycle",
